ero, a man of extravagant tastes and defiant spirit, retreats with a thousand courtiers into the secluded, fortified walls of his abbey-like castle, sealing themselves off from the suffering world outside. There, he constructs a world of opulence and distraction—lavish feasts, music, and endless entertainment—attempting to deny the reality of death and suffering through indulgence and denial. The prince’s relationship with his courtiers is one of patronage and control; they follow him not out of loyalty, but out of fear of the outside world and dependence on his protection.\\n\\nMonths pass in this artificial paradise, until Prince Prospero decides to host a grand masked ball in the seven uniquely decorated rooms of his castle, each adorned with stained-glass windows that match their color scheme. The final room, however, is black, with blood-red panes and a massive ebony clock that chimes each hour, momentarily silencing the revelry and reminding all of the passage of time and the inevitability of death. As the clock strikes midnight, a mysterious figure appears—dressed as the Red Death itself, draped in grave clothes and drenched in blood. The guests are paralyzed with dread, unable to comprehend how the plague could breach their sanctuary. Prince Prospero, consumed by rage and fear, confronts the figure with a dagger, only to be pursued through the seven rooms, culminating in the black chamber where he collapses dead upon contact.\\n\\nIn the final moment, the figure is revealed to be an empty shell, a manifestation of the plague’s true nature—inevitable, inescapable, and indifferent to wealth or power. The revelers, too, fall one by one, succumbing to the disease that had been silently spreading among them all along. The ebony clock, which had marked each passing hour, falls silent as the last guest dies. The castle, once a fortress of denial, becomes a tomb, and the Red Death claims every soul within, proving that no amount of wealth, isolation, or spectacle can shield one from fate. The story ends with the complete collapse of Prospero’s illusion of control and the triumph of death over human arrogance.\\n\\n\",\n  1483→      \"themes\": [\n  1484→        \"identity\",\n  1485→        \"ambition\",\n  1486→        \"loss\"\n  1487→      ],\n  1488→      \"narrative_style\": \"first-person retrospective\"\n  1489→    },\n  1490→    \"model_usage\": {\n  1491→      \"Chapter Detection\": {\n  1492→        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n  1493→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1494→      },\n  1495→      \"Character Extraction\": {\n  1496→        \"model\": \"qwen3-next:80b-a3b-instruct-q8_0\",\n  1497→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1498→      },\n  1499→      \"Chapter Summaries\": {\n  1500→        \"model\": \"qwen3-next:80b-a3b-instruct-q8_0\",\n  1501→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1502→      },\n  1503→      \"Character Profiles\": {\n  1504→        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n  1505→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1506→      },\n  1507→      \"Pronunciation Guide\": {\n  1508→        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n  1509→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1510→      }\n  1511→    },\n  1512→    \"timing\": {\n  1513→      \"Chapter Detection\": {\n  1514→        \"duration_seconds\": 17.16,\n  1515→        \"duration_formatted\": \"17.2s\"\n  1516→      },\n  1517→      \"Character Extraction\": {\n  1518→        \"duration_seconds\": 318.38,\n  1519→        \"duration_formatted\": \"5m 18s\"\n  1520→      },\n  1521→      \"Chapter Summaries\": {\n  1522→        \"duration_seconds\": 28.02,\n  1523→        \"duration_formatted\": \"28.0s\"\n  1524→      },\n  1525→      \"Character Profiles\": {\n  1526→        \"duration_seconds\": 26.65,\n  1527→        \"duration_formatted\": \"26.6s\"\n  1528→      },\n  1529→      \"Pronunciation Guide\": {\n  1530→        \"duration_seconds\": 75.54,\n  1531→        \"duration_formatted\": \"1m 16s\"\n  1532→      },\n  1533→      \"total\":